WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.510
The Develop phase is where the plan becomes materials.

2
00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:16.100
Course creators write narration, refine screen text, create slides, build graphics, record audio, edit video, assemble scenarios, configure interactions, create job aids, and build quizzes.

3
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:24.264
This is the most visible phase, but it works best when the analysis and design work have already clarified what needs to be built.

4
00:00:24.264 --> 00:00:29.071
Development should begin with stable content and a manageable prototype.

5
00:00:29.071 --> 00:00:37.465
A prototype might include a representative module, a sample interaction, a narration treatment, or a technical publishing test.

6
00:00:37.465 --> 00:00:47.079
The point is to validate structure, tone, visual style, accessibility approach, and technical behavior before the team invests in full production.

7
00:00:47.079 --> 00:00:51.657
Prototyping reduces the chance of rebuilding the entire course later.

8
00:00:51.657 --> 00:00:54.862
Asset organization matters during development.

9
00:00:54.862 --> 00:01:03.179
Use clear folder structures, module-based file names, version notes, and a distinction between source files and final exports.

10
00:01:03.179 --> 00:01:10.275
Keep narration scripts, audio files, caption files, graphics, authoring files, and review notes traceable.

11
00:01:10.275 --> 00:01:17.447
Maintainability is not something to worry about after launch; it starts while the materials are being created.

12
00:01:17.447 --> 00:01:20.499
Review cycles should be managed deliberately.

13
00:01:20.499 --> 00:01:33.776
Subject matter experts can verify accuracy, instructional reviewers can check flow and alignment, accessibility reviewers can identify barriers, and technical reviewers can test publishing behavior.

14
00:01:33.776 --> 00:01:42.398
Too many unstructured review comments can slow the project, so define review roles and the type of feedback needed at each checkpoint.

15
00:01:42.398 --> 00:01:45.908
Avoid overproduction before the design is validated.

16
00:01:45.908 --> 00:01:51.936
Highly polished videos, custom interactions, or detailed graphics are expensive to change.

17
00:01:51.936 --> 00:01:58.345
Build enough to test the idea, then commit to final production when the team agrees the design works.

18
00:01:58.345 --> 00:02:06.968
The goal of development is not maximum complexity; it is purposeful material that learners can use and the organization can maintain.
